{"step_index":220,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-23T19:39:07Z","content":"I have successfully implemented the layout fixes and upgraded the CAM Planner simulation!\n\nHere is a summary of the updates made to `index.html` and `app.js`:\n\n### 1. UI Layout Fixes\n- **No More Horizontal Scrolling**: I added `overflow-x-hidden` to the main body and fixed the drawer's CSS so it acts as a true absolute overlay (`fixed top-0 right-0 z-50`), completely resolving the scrollbar issue.\n- **Condensed Data Table**: The Live Tracking table now stacks the \"Primary Object\" and \"Secondary Object\" into a single **\"Objects Involved\"** column. I also stacked the Date and Time into a single **\"Approach (UTC)\"** column, freeing up a lot of horizontal space and making the table look much cleaner!\n\n### 2. Upgraded CAM Simulation\n- **Burn Direction Toggles**: Added interactive \"Prograde\" and \"Retrograde\" buttons above the slider with glowing active states.\n- **Estimated Propellant Cost**: As you move the Delta-V slider, it dynamically calculates the fuel penalty (mocked at $2.5\\text{ kg}$ per $1.0\\text{ m/s}$ of Delta-V) and displays it in real-time.\n- **New Collision Probability ($P_c$)**: I wrote a custom JS function that exponentially decays the original $P_c$ based on your thrust output. You can now watch the probability mathematically plummet from e.g., $1.12\\times 10^{-5}$ down into the safe $10^{-8}$ ranges as you drag the slider!\n- **Authorize Action**: The glowing bottom button is now wired up. Clicking it fires an `alert('Maneuver Uploaded to Ground Station.')` and smoothly closes the drawer.\n\nSince I don't have a monitor in my backend sandbox to take screenshots, **please refresh your browser at http://localhost:3000** to see it! Open the CAM drawer, drag the Delta-V slider to 1.5 m/s, and watch the Fuel Cost and $P_c$ metrics respond instantly! Let me know if the layout looks better!","thinking":"**Prioritizing Tool Usage**\n\nI'm now focusing on tool selection, understanding that choosing the right tool is key. My aim is to leverage the most specialized tool available for each specific task to avoid unnecessary complexity. The broader tools will be considered only when a specific tool does not exist. I'm carefully analyzing the available tools to ensure I pick the most efficient one.\n\n\n**Refining Tool Choice**\n\nI'm now implementing the rules and guidelines I've established for tool selection. I'm focusing on avoiding `cat`, `grep` within bash, and `ls`, `cat`, `grep`, `sed` when alternatives are better. I'm also ensuring I list all related tools before making calls, prioritizing the most specific ones. Recent updates include improvements to the HTML UI layout. I fixed the overflow issue, stacked table columns, and improved the CAM drawer UI.\n\n\n"}
{"step_index":221,"source":"USER_EXPLICIT","type":"USER_INPUT","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-23T19:42:35Z","content":"<USER_REQUEST>\n\"The 3D orbit viewer is blacking out and blurring when zooming into a satellite because the camera is clipping through the entity and looking into empty space. We also need to add more technical realism to the CAM Planner.Please update app.js and index.html with the following:1. Fix the 3D Cinematic Camera (No More Black Screens):When a user double-clicks an entity (or clicks it in the table), use viewer.flyTo(entity, { offset: new Cesium.HeadingPitchRange(0, -Cesium.Math.toRadians(30), 50000) }). This stops the camera 50 km away and tilts it 30 degrees down so the 4K Earth is always in the cinematic background.Upgrade the satellite graphics: Instead of the default blurry Cesium.PointGraphics, use Cesium.BillboardGraphics. Set the image to a glowing target reticle SVG (you can generate a data URI for a clean, futuristic circle with a dot in the middle).2. Upgrade the CAM Planner Functionality:Propulsion System Selector: In the CAM Drawer, add a dropdown or toggle for 'Propulsion Type' with two options: 'Chemical (Hydrazine)' and 'Electric (Ion)'.Dynamic Mass Penalty: Update the JS logic. If Chemical is selected, the fuel cost calculation should be high (e.g., $Cost = \\text{Delta-V} \\times 3.5\\text{ kg}$). If Electric is selected, the fuel cost is extremely low ($Cost = \\text{Delta-V} \\times 0.2\\text{ kg}$), demonstrating to the judges that you understand Specific Impulse (ISP) tradeoffs.TCA Countdown Clock: Add a prominent, red digital countdown timer in the drawer labeled 'Time to Closest Approach (TCA)'. Calculate the time difference between Date.now() and the conjunction's UTC timestamp. Write a setInterval function to make the clock visually tick down in real-time (e.g., T-Minus 04:12:35).Action: Write the updated code. Wait for the server to refresh. First, verify the 3D globe zooms in cinematically without blackin